# The synthetic fixtures, and what each one exercises.
#
# Generated by tools/fixtures/make_fixtures.py in the pinned fonttools image.
# Do not edit either this file or the fonts beside it: `make check-fixtures`
# regenerates them and fails on a byte difference.
#
# Every font here is drawn from outlines in that script, carries this
# repository's copyright, and is LGPL-3.0-only like the code around it. No
# third-party font is committed to this repository at all; the real fonts a
# differential needs live only in the oracle image (design.md section 14.5).
#
# Tab-separated: <filename> <what it exercises>.
arabic-join.ttf	TrueType: eighteen Arabic letters with isol/init/medi/fina forms drawn as joined bars, lam-alef and beh-noon ligatures in rlig, a tatweel and a fatha, for kashida justification
bare-matrix.cff	A bare CFF whose FontMatrix scales the two axes differently, so it states no em this library can report and is refused by name; its translation components carry a DICT real in exponent form and one with more mantissa digits than 16.16 holds, and it states its licence in Notice rather than Copyright
bare.cff	A bare CFF font program with no sfnt around it: the same CFF table as cff-curves.otf, so the two faces must draw identically
basic.ttf	TrueType, cmap format 4 on (0,3) and (3,1), OS/2 v4, post v3
bitmap-gz.pcf.gz	The same PCF as bitmap.pcf inside gzip, which is how every PCF in the world ships: it must read to identical glyphs, because the wrapper decides nothing about what the font is
bitmap-ink.bdf	BDF: what only a per-glyph box can say - a negative x offset, a descender whose BBX y is below the baseline, advances narrower and wider than the box, ENCODING -1 for a glyph with no character, an empty BBX with no BITMAP rows at all, and a row whose bits past the glyph's width are set, which a reader has to clear
bitmap-lsb.pcf	PCF laid out the other way in every dimension: bits and bytes least-significant first, a two-byte scan unit, rows padded to four, compressed metrics and an ink-metrics table nothing reads - and the same glyphs as bitmap.pcf, which is the only check on that arithmetic
bitmap-swap.pcf	PCF whose bits run least-significant-first and whose bytes run most, with a four-byte scan unit: the one combination where a scan unit's bytes have to be reversed as well as its bits, which no other fixture reaches and which Pillow cannot read
bitmap-v1.psf	PSF 1: 512 glyphs, which only one bit of the mode byte says, and a Unicode table in UCS-2 little-endian with 0xFFFE sequence separators
bitmap-wide.hex	.hex at the other two widths the format allows: a 16-pixel glyph and a 32-pixel one, and a six-digit codepoint past the BMP
bitmap.bdf	BDF: the shared design, with the properties that state the baseline (FONT_ASCENT, FONT_DESCENT), the pixel size, the family and the copyright, and a name per character
bitmap.hex	GNU Unifont .hex: eight glyphs of 8x16 in hexadecimal, one per line, with no header, no baseline and no names - the same design as bitmap.psf, bitmap.bdf and bitmap.pcf, so all four must read to identical pixels
bitmap.pcf	PCF: the shared design, most-significant bit and byte first, rows padded to one byte, uncompressed metrics, with properties, BDF accelerators, encodings, scalable widths and glyph names
bitmap.psf	PSF 2: the shared design with a Unicode table in UTF-8, where one cell answers two codepoints and one carries a two-codepoint sequence that a reader must walk past rather than map
cff-arith.otf	CFF: the arithmetic operators computing the coordinates they draw with - div, the 255 fixed-point operand, dup, exch, drop, index, put, get, eq, not, mul, add and ifelse; no random, which is refused
cff-cid-names.otf	CFF: CID-keyed, two Font DICTs each named by a string of its own (FontName), for the subsetter's rename
cff-cid.otf	CFF: CID-keyed - ROS, an FDArray of two Font DICTs with different Private DICTs and different local subroutines, FDSelect format 3, and a charset holding CIDs rather than SIDs
cff-cid3.otf	CFF: CID-keyed with three Font DICTs of different defaultWidthX, the glyphs split one to a DICT after .notdef, for a subset that drops the middle DICT
cff-curves.otf	CFF: every curve operator in the form a pen never writes - three-group hvcurveto and vhcurveto with the trailing operand, hhcurveto and vvcurveto with the leading one, rcurveline, rlinecurve, and all four of flex, hflex, hflex1 and flex1 (twice, once per axis)
cff-hints.otf	CFF: stem hints and the masks their count sizes - an odd operand count carrying the advance, a two-byte mask, stems declared inside a subroutine, an implicit vstem before hintmask, cntrmask, and a width on vstem
cff-names.otf	CFF: a Top DICT stating FullName and FamilyName as strings of its own, after the glyph names, for the subsetter's rename
cff-repeats.otf	CFF: twelve glyphs sharing two path fragments, six hinted glyphs sharing stems, masks and fragments, a long run of one stroke, and one glyph that shares nothing, for the subsetter's subroutines
cff-seac.otf	CFF: an accented character (endchar with four operands) named by Standard Encoding code, a charset in format 1, a name in the font's own String INDEX, and a custom encoding with a supplement
cff-subrs.otf	CFF: local and global subroutines with the bias applied, nesting, a subroutine that ends without return, defaultWidthX, a nominalWidthX delta, and one glyph whose charstring advance hmtx disagrees with
cff-subset.otf	CFF: eight and sixteen stems before a hintmask (mask width on a multiple-of-eight boundary), three glyphs named in the font's own String INDEX, and a subroutine call, for the subsetter
cff-type1.otf	CFF with CharstringType 1: Type 1 programs - hsbw, sbw, closepath, seac with its side-bearing correction, flex and hint replacement through callothersubr and pop, div, dotsection, hstem3 and setcurrentpoint
cff.otf	OTTO flavour: a CFF outline table, every metric table unchanged
cff2-fds.otf	CFF2: 300 Font DICTs, one glyph in each, each with its own StdHW, so FDSelect is in format 4 (formats 0 and 3 cannot name past 256)
cmap-format0.ttf	cmap format 0 on (1,0): the 256-byte glyph array
cmap-format12.ttf	cmap format 12 on (3,10) past the BMP, beside a format 4
cmap-format6.ttf	cmap format 6 on (1,0): a trimmed array with firstCode
cmap-symbol.ttf	cmap (3,0) symbol only: U+0041 reachable only via the 0xF0xx rule
collection.ttc	ttcf: two faces sharing tables, with different OS/2 versions
color-cbdt-bgra.ttf	CBDT with uncompressed premultiplied BGRA pictures under EBDT image formats 1, 2, 5, 6 and 7 (and a composite, 8, which is declined), at a strike depth of 32 and under index formats 1, 3 and 5
color-cbdt-deep.ttf	CBDT with 16-bit PNGs written by hand (RGBA, RGB, grey, grey with alpha) and one 8-bit one in the same strike: a PNG's depth is the picture's, and the decoded samples are exactly the ones written
color-cbdt-metrics.ttf	CBDT with two strikes (16 and 7 ppem) of 127 glyphs whose metrics sweep the byte ranges, over flat-color pictures of the size the metrics state: the scaling of a picture's box to the em, and its rounding, are what is held (a half falls at 16 ppem in a 1000-unit em)
color-cbdt-only.ttf	color-cbdt.ttf with its glyf and loca removed: a font of pictures and no outlines, as Noto Color Emoji is
color-cbdt-sizes.ttf	CBDT with four strikes - 24 by 12, 20 by 20, a second 24 by 12 and 10 by 30 ppem - over glyphs with and without outlines: which strike a size picks (the smallest at least as large as the larger ppem, else the largest, the first of equals), which ppem scales which axis, and a picture winning over an outline while a glyph the chosen strike lacks keeps its outline
color-cbdt.ttf	CBLC and CBDT: two color strikes holding PNG pictures, under index formats 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 and image formats 17, 18 and 19, with a glyph in range and absent, bearings of both signs, and every PNG color type the encoder writes (RGBA, RGB, palette with transparency, grey, grey with alpha, one bit)
color-cpal0.ttf	The same COLR layers over a CPAL version 0, which has no types or labels, and two palettes that share one run of color records
color-sbix-dupes.ttf	sbix with a chain of dupe records twelve long (HarfBuzz follows eight hops), a dupe of itself, and a dupe whose target id is above 255, so it needs both bytes of the glyph id
color-sbix-metrics.ttf	sbix with two strikes (16 and 7 ppem) of 127 PNGs whose widths, heights and origin offsets sweep a range: the scaling of a picture's box to the em and its rounding are what is held
color-sbix.ttf	sbix: two strikes (40 then 20 ppem) of PNG, JPEG and TIFF pictures, a dupe of a picture, a dupe of a dupe, a dupe of itself, a mask record, and a glyph with no bytes; the same glyph is a different picture in each strike
color-v0-edges.ttf	COLR version 0 layers HarfBuzz measures by what they draw: a drawn layer beside an empty one, a glyph of only empty layers, a layer that is a composite scaled by a fractional factor, and hmtx side bearings that are not the outlines' xMin
color-v0.ttf	COLR version 0 over CPAL version 1: three base glyphs of three, one and two layers, one layer painted in the foreground color (0xFFFF), three palettes of three colors with every alpha, light and dark usability flags, palette labels with one missing and entry labels with one missing - written by fontTools, which is also the oracle
color-v1-var.ttf	COLR version 1 in a variable font: the Var forms of every paint that has one, color lines whose stops vary, and a clip box that varies, over an item variation store of weight and width regions, read through the packed outer/inner index
color-v1-varmap.ttf	The same graph with a delta-set index map that sends each VarIndexBase to the store's rows in reverse, so a reader that ignored the map would read the wrong delta
color-v1-varnone.ttf	The same graph with a delta-set index map whose every entry is 0xFFFF:0xFFFF, the map's own 'no variation', so a moved location reads the stored numbers (Google's test-glyph font leaves its fixed fields this way, and a reader that sent the entry to the store fails)
color-v1.ttf	COLR version 1: every non-variable paint format once - layers (sharing the layer list), solid, linear, radial and sweep gradients with pad, repeat and reflect color lines, glyph, color glyph, transform, translate, scale in four forms, rotate in two, skew in two, and a composite - with a clip list sharing one box across three glyphs
layout-gpos.shape	The runs layout-gpos.ttf is shaped on, one per line: script, language, features, code points, and what the case exercises
layout-gpos.ttf	GPOS with one of every lookup type and format: single in both formats, pair in both (with a value for each glyph of a pair, and a value with device tables), cursive in left-to-right and right-to-left lookups, mark to base, to ligature (a different anchor on each component) and to mark, context in formats 1, 2 and 3, chain in formats 2 and 3, and a lookup behind an extension - written by fontTools
layout-gsub.shape	The runs layout-gsub.ttf is shaped on, one per line: script, language, features, code points, and what the case exercises
layout-gsub.ttf	GSUB with one of every lookup type and format: single in both formats, multiple (including to nothing), alternate, ligature, context in formats 1, 2 and 3, chain in formats 1, 2 and 3, reverse chaining, a lookup behind an extension, lookup flags with and without a mark filtering set and a mark attachment type, and nested lookups that grow the run - written by fontTools
math-variable.ttf	TrueType: math.ttf with variation indices in four MATH device tables and a GDEF store of two sets of three rows, for pruning and renumbering the store
math.ttf	TrueType: a MATH table with every part present (constants and some with device tables, italics corrections, top accents, extended shapes, kerns on all four corners, vertical constructions with and without an assembly, a horizontal one), for the subsetter
name-mac-encodings.ttf	name: every single-byte Macintosh encoding, all 128 high bytes each
name-macroman.ttf	name: a Macintosh Roman record holding U+2122 as byte 0xAA
os2-v0.ttf	OS/2 version 0: no code page ranges, no x-height, no optical size
os2-v1.ttf	OS/2 version 1: code page ranges and nothing later
os2-v2.ttf	OS/2 version 2: x-height, cap height, default and break char
os2-v3.ttf	OS/2 version 3: version 2's fields with version 3's fsType rules
os2-v4.ttf	OS/2 version 4: the common case in the oracle corpus
os2-v5.ttf	OS/2 version 5: optical point sizes, stored as twentieths
outline-broken-loca.ttf	loca: the last entry running backwards, so that exactly one glyph is corrupt and the rest of the font still answers (M11)
outline-composite.ttf	glyf composites: byte and word offsets, all three transform encodings, scaled and unscaled offsets, point matching below and above the signed-byte boundary, nesting, USE_MY_METRICS
outline-cubic-flag.ttf	glyf: flag bit 0x80 in a font declaring glyphDataFormat 0, so the per-glyph refusal is the only one that can fire
outline-cubic.ttf	glyf: head.glyphDataFormat 1, the cubic extension this library refuses by name rather than mis-drawing
outline-loca-long.ttf	loca: head.indexToLocFormat 1, so offsets are stored whole rather than halved
outline-simple.ttf	glyf: an explicit quadratic, an implied midpoint, a contour starting off-curve, the same contour stored from another point of its cycle, an all-off-curve contour, a one-point contour, instructions, a 200-point contour, an arch whose control box is not its curve, and a bar whose two edges are each a 259-long run of one repeated flag
post-v1.ttf	post format 1.0: all 258 standard glyphs, no stored names
post-v2.ttf	post format 2.0: standard indices including 257, and one extra name
post-v3.ttf	post format 3.0: a header that states it has no names
strike-composite.ttf	EBDT image formats 8 and 9: composite glyphs, each paired with a non-composite twin of the same strike that draws the same pixels - nothing in Debian has a composite and fontTools reads the component list without composing it, so the identity is the only check there is. A format 8 header with its pad byte and a format 9 one without, a composite of a composite, a composite with zero components, leaf bearings that all differ from the composites' so that using them would show, and components at columns 5 and 9 so the blit is not byte-aligned
strike-formats.ttf	One EBLC strike whose six index subtables use six different index/image format pairings - 4-byte and 2-byte offsets, sparse glyph lists, constant metrics, small and big metrics, byte- and bit-aligned rows - all drawing one design, so the bytes differ everywhere and the pixels must not. Covers every cell of the grid the real population does not: image format 6 appears only in files fontTools cannot read, and index formats 3, 4 and 5 appear nowhere in Debian
strikes.ttf	Three EBLC strikes - 10, 12 and 16 ppem - of four glyphs each, index subtable format 2 with image format 5, which is 97.8% of the index subtables in the only two Debian packages that carry this table. The first fixture in this library with more than one strike, so the first input where GFNT_STRIKE_NEAREST has to choose; 11 ppem is a tie between two of them, and glyph 0 is in none of them
svg-docs.ttf	An SVG table of four documents: a gzip one for glyph 0, a plain one for glyphs 2 and 3, a gzip one for glyph 4 and a plain one with a non-ASCII character for glyphs 6 and 7, with glyph 5 and everything past 7 in none - written by fontTools, which is also the oracle
type1-big.pfb	A Type 1 program larger than one allocation: 80 glyphs, subroutine numbers 0, 1 and 40 with a gap between, and a glyph that calls the high one - the three reallocation paths no other fixture reaches
type1-gz.pfb.gz	A gzipped Type 1 program - two derivations stacked, inflate then eexec - which nobody ships and which is the only input where each layer has to free the one it consumed
type1.pfa	The same Type 1 font as ASCII: hex after eexec, no segment framing, the -| |- | spellings, /lenIV 0, a /CharStrings count smaller than the glyphs it defines, and StandardEncoding
type1.pfb	A Type 1 font program in PFB framing: binary segments with little-endian lengths, eexec, a per-charstring cipher, a custom /Encoding, and the same charstrings as cff-type1.otf
variable-avar2.ttf	variable-gvar.ttf's font with an avar version 2: segment maps, then a variation store of three regions (weight up, width up, weight down) that moves each axis as a function of the others, and an index map that sends the weight axis to the second row and the width axis to the first, written by fontTools
variable-cff2-hvar.otf	CFF2: HVAR with a permuted advance map and a shared left-bearing map, VVAR with advance-height and vertical-origin maps, and MVAR, for the subsetter's renumbering
variable-cff2-implicit.otf	CFF2: HVAR with no advance map (the glyph id is the row) and a shared left-bearing map, for the subsetter's renumbering
variable-cff2.otf	a CFF2 variable font written by fontTools: two axes, three regions (weight up, weight down, weight and width up together) and glyphs that blend one value, several at once, a curve with a fractional delta, an hvcurveto, and a hintmask
variable-cvar.ttf	variable-gvar.ttf's font with a cvt table of eight values and a cvar of four tuples: every value, a sparse list, an intermediate region and a two-axis corner, written by fontTools
variable-featurevars-nested.ttf	variable-featurevars.ttf's font with its FeatureVariations conditions in formats 3, 4 and 5 (and, or, not), nested, for the subsetter's rewrite
variable-featurevars.shape	The runs variable-featurevars.ttf is shaped on, each at a location in its design space: one per FeatureVariations record, the order they are tried in, and either side of a condition's edge
variable-featurevars.ttf	variable-gvar.ttf's font with a GSUB 1.1 whose FeatureVariations has three records: one condition, two that must both hold, and a substitution of two glyphs; written by fontTools
variable-gvar.ttf	fvar, avar and gvar written by fontTools: two axes (one hidden), three named instances (one with a PostScript name), a bent avar on one axis and an identity map on the other; and in gvar a single named point, an inferred run between two corners, named points in two contours of one glyph, an intermediate region, a two-axis corner, shared and embedded peaks, a composite whose component offsets move (one placed by matching points, whose delta is ignored), a composite of a composite, and a glyph with no contours that varies only in its phantom points
variable-hvar.ttf	variable-gvar.ttf's glyphs with HVAR - an advance mapping and a left bearing mapping that shares three rows among nine glyphs, over two regions - and MVAR carrying the hhea, window and typographic ascent, descent and gap, all written by fontTools
variable-stat.ttf	variable-gvar.ttf's font with a STAT version 1.2 holding every axis value format: a linked value (elidable), a plain value, a range around a nominal value, and a value on two axes at once, written by fontTools
vertical-text.ttf	TrueType: vhea and vmtx with vertical advances unlike the horizontal ones, a vert feature giving CJK punctuation vertical forms, ideographs and kana, Latin letters, digits and brackets, for vertical paragraph layout
woff-basic.woff	basic.ttf as a WOFF 1 written by fontTools, tables zlib-compressed one at a time (some stored, because compressing did not help): the reader rebuilds the sfnt, so every answer is basic.ttf's
woff-stored.woff	basic.ttf in a WOFF 1 whose every table is stored uncompressed, written by this generator rather than by fontTools: the compressed length equals the original length for every table, which is the arm a compressing writer rarely takes
woff2-basic.woff2	basic.ttf as a WOFF 2 written by fontTools with its default transforms: one Brotli stream, glyf and loca transformed, the directory in tag order (so head sits between them, which the specification forbids and fontTools does anyway)
woff2-collection.woff2	A WOFF 2 collection of two faces that share every table but name and head, with the shared glyf and loca transformed
woff2-hmtx.woff2	basic.ttf as a WOFF 2 whose hmtx is transformed too, written by fontTools: the bearings are kept because basic.ttf's are not its glyphs' xMin
woff2-stored.woff2	basic.ttf as a WOFF 2 with every table in its null transform - glyf and loca as version 3 - followed by a metadata block and a private block, written by this generator
woff2-triplets-source.ttf	The font woff2-triplets.woff2 was made from, to compare it with
woff2-triplets.woff2	A WOFF 2 from this generator's own writer: all 128 triplet classes on and off the curve, a composite glyph with instructions and a scale, explicit bounding boxes, an overlap bitmap, and an hmtx with both bearing arrays dropped; fontTools reads it back to the source font before it is written
